!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
The projectional editor
description: Editing structure instead of text, one cell at a time
- There is no source textarea
  notes: |
    TODO — unlock a node and show that the rendered surface IS the editable one: a tree of
    cells that mirror the syntax tree, not a text box beside a preview.
  - |
    The rendered surface **is** the editable surface — a tree of cells over the syntax
    tree, with no source box beside it.
- Structure appears as you type
  notes: |
    TODO — type `k:` and watch the pair form; type `[` and watch the closer be projected
    and the caret land inside. The grammar decides what a keystroke means.
  - |
    TODO — one sentence: typing a delimiter materializes the structure it opens.
- The level rule
  notes: |
    TODO — docs/server/yamlover-editor. Enter after committing a value DESCENDS into it; a
    sibling at the same level costs a Shift-Tab. Type three levels, then climb back out.
  - |
    **Enter descends** into what you just wrote; **Shift-Tab** climbs back out. A sibling
    costs one Shift-Tab.
- Every edit is a surgical op
  notes: |
    TODO — show the ops going over the wire (or just say it): the editor sends emplace,
    insert, remove against a path — it never rewrites the whole file. Which is why an
    external edit and a UI edit can land in the same file.
  - |
    TODO — one sentence on surgical edits versus whole-file writes.
- Storage moves under you
  notes: |
    TODO — add a child to a leaf and watch it promote into its own directory, named with
    an order number. The value did not change; where it lives did.
  - |
    Content that outgrows a line **moves storage by itself** — a promoted node becomes its
    own directory, order-numbered by title.
- Two projections, one model
  notes: |
    TODO — the same node as a chapter and as data: switch tabs and edit in both. Same
    model underneath, two ways of seeing it.
  - |
    TODO — one sentence.
- What keeps it honest
  notes: |
    TODO — the edit corpus: keystroke scripts replayed through the real server, asserting
    the file round-trips (edit-examples/). Say why that matters for a data editor: a
    keystroke with nowhere to go is a bug, not a shrug.
  - |
    Keystroke scripts are replayed through the real server and the file must **round-trip**
    — see `edit-examples/`.
